    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec required assigning a `membershipNumber` formatted `<customerCode>-M<YY>` (YY = last two digits of the registration year). I followed the existing \"assign\" step pattern rather than bolting logic onto an existing class:\n\n1. **New function class** \u2014 `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber` (single `service` method, single responsibility): reads the already-set `customerCode` and `registrationDate` off the `Owner` and formats `%s-M%02d` with `year % 100`. It mirrors the sibling `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`/`AssignHouseholdId` classes.\n\n2. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted an `assignMembershipNumber` step into `owners.POST.yml` right after `assignCustomerCode` (so the code exists) and before `save` (so it persists), under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **Entity** \u2014 added the `membershipNumber` field + `membership_number` column mapping and accessors to `Owner`.\n\n4. **Schema** \u2014 added `membership_number` to all four DB schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, h2, postgres, mysql) so the persisted value round-trips.\n\n5. **API contract** \u2014 added the `membershipNumber` read-only property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`; the generated `OwnerDto` + MapStruct mapper pick it up by name automatically, so the fetched owner exposes it.\n\n`AcceptanceTest.coreMembershipNumberFormat` passes, and `mvn test` reports `BUILD SUCCESS` (1 test, 0 failures) \u2014 it creates an owner, re-fetches from the DB, and confirms `membershipNumber == customerCode + \"-M\" + YY`.",
